 @clansome  no, it won’t give you more cards on the market. At least not better than current situation. Why? It is because any die that do not make it into 3080 Ti can be made into 3080 instead. And do you see more original 3080 10GB right now? The entire reason why Nvidia made this card, is because they put the MSRP of the original 10GB card way too low so this is basically price reset for them. Mark my word that you will see way less of 3080 10GB and more of 3080 12GB even if it exist.

You do not need to say the last line. Of course it will sell, and Nvidia knows it. That’s why they make sure to tack-in extra 200$-300$ for them in MSRP so even if the market goes to “normal” it will never be back when the original 30-series launch. And since they do not have “Founder’s Edition”, Nvidia make sure to screw its partner too so they can deflect blame to  partners if price is too high.

You can argue that corporation need to maximize profit, and even if its true, there are good ways, and scummy ways to do it. This one is arguably very scummy

 @ArchusKanzaki  Not scummy, that is a typical salty gamer attitude imo. Everything is "scummy" or the "companies don't think about the gamers" mentality is quite frankly a very narrow perspective on how graphics cards (or any product for that matter) should be sold. Those cards are not "ours", made solely for gaming (or whatever the function used may be), they are PRODUCT, nothing more, nothing less. OK I have a 3080, I'm very lucky and I paid MSRP buying it in a configured prebuilt by a company I trust; sure I had to wait 4 months but I got it. I don't blame Nvidia one bit for taking a mark up on a newer variant. I don't think AIB's do take the blame, they just want to be able to get components to make cards to sell, they don't care. You won't see any of them going out of business.

From speaking to a local computer shop shortly after launch, he was telling me that the main people making the mark-up (on the retail side of things) were the wholesalers. They were selling cards to the highest bidders back then. His wholesaler also supplies a very large etailer where I am in the UK, in fact the one I bought my PC through. The price that they offered was no different, if they wanted one they had to pay and you were talking £1000 for a 3080 back then wholesale. I would imagine that he could offload his cards to scalpers or miners if the retail outlets didn't want to pay. Neither Nvidia or any of the AIBs saw any of that extra money but they all took the heat. Eventually they (the etailer) dealt direct with AIBs through a deal with Nvidia themselves hence my card being MSRP. I was offered an option for a 3080Ti on the week of their launch which would have saved me a month off that wait but I wasn't prepared to pay an extra £500 even back then. 

The card plays nicely in my gaming rig and won't be updated for at least 5 years and I haven't even mined on it (which is a shame as I could have paid off whole PC by now). Ah well. But you're right in that it's not a clever move and they need to change their naming system too, shades of 1060 3Gb/6Gb again.

 @cppctek  I will repeat that to you one more time since you don't seams to get it.
The only reason this GPU exist is to get rid of MSRP and sell this card at whatever price they want.
There is no supply change, no yealding improvement, nothing. Just two more GB of the same memory and no FE models, so they can set the price whatever the f**k they please.
